# Research sources and notes

## Downloaded papers

Whenever you download a paper for research (arXiv `.tex`, HTML, PDF, or extracted text), keep it in a dedicated subfolder of `research/`. Do not leave sources in the repo root, in `paper/`, in `util/`, or in a temp directory.

- Name the folder after the citation key in the bib file, lowercase and compact (`research/hogg2010/`, `research/foremanmackey2013/`).
- Keep the `.tex` (and any `.bbl` or macros needed to read it) or the extracted text. Skip large ancillary files unless they are needed to verify a claim.
- A downloaded third-party source is never part of a public snapshot. `util/push_public.py` skips every `research/` subfolder.

## Notes

- Working notes (a derivation, an injection test, a literature summary, a decision and its reasons) are `research/<topic>.md`. Write them in the same style as the paper.
- Do not paste a downloaded paper into a note; quote at most the sentence you need and give the citation key.
- Referee reports and responses are `research/review<N>.md` and `research/response<N>.md`. They stay private: `push_public.py` never copies `review*.md`.

## Claims about other work

When the manuscript says what another paper found, check it against that paper's text (download it into `research/<key>/` if needed), not against memory or a citing paper.
